--TEST--
`Core\Reflect::forClass` returns `null` for a class the compiler made and for a `Core` class, and `construct` refuses each of those, an `abstract` class and an interface, while it still builds a plain class and an error class — `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Counter
{
    public static function upTo(int $n): Iterator<int>
    {
        yield $n;
    }
}

class Maker
{
    public function adder(): callable
    {
        return fn(int $x): int => $x + 1;
    }
}

abstract class Shape
{
}

interface Named
{
}

class Point
{
    public int $x;

    public function constructor(int $x)
    {
        $this->x = $x;
    }
}

class Probe
{
    // Prints what `forClass` returns for `$class`.
    public static function find(string $class): void
    {
        var $info = Core\Reflect::forClass($class);
        if ($info == null) {
            echo $class, ": null\n";
        } else {
            echo $class, ": described\n";
        }
    }

    // Calls `construct` on `$info` and prints the result or the error.
    public static function build(Core\Reflect\ClassInfo $info, array<mixed> $arguments): void
    {
        try {
            mixed $made = $info->construct($arguments);
            echo 'made ', Core\Reflect::forObject($made)->name(), "\n";
        } catch (LogicError $e) {
            echo $e->message, "\n";
        }
    }
}

// The names of a generator's class, a function value's class and an anonymous object's class.
var $anonymous = { a: 1 };
string $generator = Core\Reflect::forObject(Counter::upTo(1))->name();
string $function = Core\Reflect::forObject((new Maker())->adder())->name();
string $shape = Core\Reflect::forObject($anonymous)->name();

array<string> $names = [$generator, $function, $shape, 'Core\Http\TlsInfo', 'Shape', 'Named', 'Point'];
foreach ($names as string $name) {
    Probe::find($name);
}

// `forObject` describes each object, and `construct` on that description throws.
Probe::build(Core\Reflect::forObject(Counter::upTo(1)), []);
Probe::build(Core\Reflect::forObject((new Maker())->adder()), []);
Probe::build(Core\Reflect::forObject($anonymous), []);

// `forClass` still describes an `abstract` class and an interface. `construct` throws for both.
var $abstract = Core\Reflect::forClass('Shape');
if ($abstract != null) {
    Probe::build($abstract, []);
}
var $interface = Core\Reflect::forClass('Named');
if ($interface != null) {
    Probe::build($interface, []);
}

// A class the program declares, and an error class, are still built.
// The constructor of `LogicError` has two parameters: the message and the previous error.
var $point = Core\Reflect::forClass('Point');
if ($point != null) {
    Probe::build($point, [3]);
}
var $error = Core\Reflect::forClass('LogicError');
if ($error != null) {
    Probe::build($error, ['built', null]);
}
--EXPECT--
Counter::upTo$gen: null
Maker$fn0: null
$shape{a}: null
Core\Http\TlsInfo: null
Shape: described
Named: described
Point: described
Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::construct(): `Counter::upTo$gen` is not a class this program declares, so there is nothing to construct
Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::construct(): `Maker$fn0` is not a class this program declares, so there is nothing to construct
Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::construct(): `$shape{a}` is not a class this program declares, so there is nothing to construct
Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::construct(): `Shape` is `abstract` or an interface, so it has no instances
Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::construct(): `Named` is `abstract` or an interface, so it has no instances
made Point
made LogicError
